Extra PHP versions (8.2 - 8.4) for Sentora on Ubuntu 26.04
==========================================================

(Debian 13: the same scripts build sentora-php-<version>-debian13-amd64.tar.gz when run on Debian 13,
for 8.2, 8.3 and 8.5 (Debian's own PHP is 8.4) and 7.1 (without the imap add-on: Debian 13 has no
c-client). The "PHP bundles" workflow of this repository (Actions > PHP bundles > Run workflow) builds
them in a Debian 13 container and puts them on the "current" release; give it the PHP versions to
build, such as "8.5.12 8.3.36 8.2.35 7.1". sentora-php-install on a Debian server downloads the
debian13 bundle. The rest of this file applies to both, with "debian13" for "ubuntu26.04".)

The installer downloads sentora-php-<version>-ubuntu26.04-amd64.tar.gz from the
GitHub release of RusTus/sentora-installers-2027 and installs each in /opt/sentora-php/<version>/ as the service
sentora-php<version>-fpm. These builds are not updated by apt: when PHP
publishes a release for one of them (https://www.php.net/releases/), rebuild
and update it with the two scripts here.

1. Build (as root on an Ubuntu 26.04 amd64 machine, ideally a spare VM, since
   it installs compilers and -dev packages):

       bash build-sentora-php.sh 8.3.37

   It downloads php-8.3.37.tar.xz from php.net, checks its SHA-256 against
   php.net, builds it with the same options as the shipped bundles, checks it
   runs, and writes sentora-php-8.3-ubuntu26.04-amd64.tar.gz in the current
   folder. Compiling takes 10-30 minutes depending on the machine.

2. Publish: attach the .tar.gz to the "current" sentora-installers-2027 release on
   GitHub, replacing the old file (new installs and step 3 use it).

3. Update a running server (as root on that server):

       sentora-php-install 8.3

   (installed with the panel; for a version the server already has, it shows the
   installed and the new build and asks first; --yes skips the question.
   update-sentora-php.sh in this folder does the same, for older panels.)

   It downloads the bundle from the "current" GitHub release (TAG=<tag> picks
   another), checks its SHA-256 against the release, checks the new build runs with the
   server's current settings, swaps the program files (keeping etc/: php.ini,
   php-fpm.conf, the pool), restarts sentora-php8.3-fpm, and puts the old
   files back if it does not start. Domains on that version are only offline
   for the few seconds of the restart.

Security support (PHP project):
   8.1  ended 31 Dec 2025 - no more releases, nothing to rebuild
   8.2  until 31 Dec 2026
   8.3  until 31 Dec 2027
   8.4  until 31 Dec 2028

PHP 7.1 - only for moving old Sentora 2.0.2 websites
====================================================

PHP 7.1 has had no security fixes since 1 Dec 2019. It is offered only so that websites
moved from an old Sentora 2.0.2 server that do not run on PHP 8 yet keep working while
they are fixed. The installer installs it switched OFF and does not start it; an
administrator switches it on in PHP Versions for a move (the page asks first), and should
move each site to PHP 8 and switch 7.1 off again.

Build (as root, on Ubuntu 24.04 or 26.04 amd64):

       bash build-sentora-php71.sh

It builds PHP 7.1.33 (the last 7.1 release) with its own OpenSSL 1.1.1w, curl 7.88.1,
libxml2 2.9.14, libmcrypt 2.5.8, OpenLDAP 2.6.10 (GnuTLS), ICU 64.2 (intl) and libxslt
1.1.34 (xsl) compiled in (PHP 7.1 does not build against Ubuntu 26.04's OpenSSL 3, libxml2
or ICU), checks every source against its published checksum or signature, and writes
sentora-php-7.1-ubuntu26.04-amd64.tar.gz. Besides the C and C++ libraries it links only
zlib, libpng16, libjpeg-turbo, freetype, libwebp, gmp and GnuTLS, which are the same on
Ubuntu 24.04 and 26.04.

Add-ons, each its own file in lib/php/extensions: apcu 5.1.21, redis 5.3.7, memcached
3.2.0, imagick 3.7.0 and imap. They use Ubuntu's own libmemcached, ImageMagick and c-client
(which bring Ubuntu's OpenSSL 3 and libxml2), so PHP is linked with --exclude-libs,ALL: its
compiled-in libraries stay private to it and never meet Ubuntu's copies in one process. The
build stops if php-fpm still offers one of their functions, or if an add-on does not load.
bin/sentora-php-addons in the bundle installs the Ubuntu libraries and switches on only the
add-ons that load; the installer and sentora-php-install run it.

KEEP_WORK=1 keeps the build's work folder (/tmp/sentora-php71-build.*), to look at a step
that failed or run it again by hand.

Attach the bundle to the "current" release like the other bundles: the one titled
"Sentora-<version> (current)" and marked Latest (or open
https://github.com/RusTus/sentora-installers-2027/releases/tag/current), Edit, replace the
file, Update release.

Add it to a server installed before it (as root):

       sentora-php-install 7.1

PHP 7.4 and 8.1 - steps for old apps on their way to a supported PHP
====================================================================

Old apps are upgraded through their own versions one at a time, and those need the PHP in
between (osTicket 1.14-1.15, Snipe-IT 5 and Magento 2.3 run on PHP 7.4; Snipe-IT 6 and
Magento 2.4.4 on PHP 8.1). Neither gets security fixes any more (7.4 since 28 Nov 2022, 8.1
since 31 Dec 2025): the installer does not install them, sentora-php-install adds them switched
OFF, and the PHP Versions page marks them and asks before switching them on.

PHP 7.4 (as root, on Ubuntu 26.04 amd64):

       bash build-sentora-php74.sh

The PHP 7.1 recipe for PHP 7.4.33 (the last 7.4 release): its own OpenSSL 1.1.1w, curl
7.88.1, libxml2 2.9.14, OpenLDAP 2.6.10, ICU 64.2 and libxslt 1.1.34 compiled in and kept
private (--exclude-libs,ALL); Ubuntu's own oniguruma, libzip, libsodium, libargon2 and sqlite
(libzip brings Ubuntu's OpenSSL 3 along, which the private copy never meets); PHP 7.4 finds
the compiled-in libraries with "pkg-config --static", the add-ons with plain pkg-config. No
mcrypt (PHP 7.2 dropped it). The same five add-ons and checks as PHP 7.1; the library check
looks at what php-fpm links itself (readelf), not at what Ubuntu's libraries bring along.

PHP 8.1 (as root, on Ubuntu 26.04 amd64):

       bash build-sentora-php.sh 8.1.34

The PHP 8.x recipe: PHP 8.1 builds on Ubuntu 26.04's own OpenSSL 3, libxml2 and ICU. For 8.1
it also builds the add-ons (apcu, redis, memcached, imagick, imap) and bin/sentora-php-addons;
ADDONS=1 builds them for another version too (imap is part of PHP up to 8.3 only).

Attach both bundles to the "current" release as for the others, then on a server:

       sentora-php-install 7.4
       sentora-php-install 8.1
